---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Glance from "../../components/Glance.astro";
import Stat from "../../components/Stat.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="session-replay"
  team="2 PMs, 9 engineers, a content writer & a support designer"
  externalLabel="newrelic.com"
  externalHref="https://newrelic.com/platform/session-replay"
  coverPlain
  readTime="6 min read"
>
  <!-- Top metrics strip -->
  <div
    slot="metrics"
    class="mx-auto mt-12 grid max-w-5xl gap-5 px-6 sm:mt-16 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:px-10"
  >
    <Stat value="1,000+" label="Active accounts & 7M weekly sessions within six months." />
    <Stat value="$3.52M" label="Monthly recurring revenue — a core Browser pillar." />
    <Stat value="Minutes → seconds" label="From symptom to root cause, in one view." />
  </div>

  <Glance>
    <Fragment slot="challenge">
      When front-end developers troubleshoot, they need to know both what the
      user was doing and how the application responded. New Relic previously
      offered only data-centric logs and traces, forcing developers to translate
      lengthy text into a best guess of user behaviour.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="strategy">
      A unified diagnostic experience that synchronises three contexts into a
      single, real-time frame of reference: the visual replay, the behavioural
      event trail, and the technical session traces.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="impact">
      Scaled to 1,000+ active accounts and 7M weekly sessions within six months,
      generating $3.52M in monthly recurring revenue and cutting time-to-insight
      from minutes to seconds.
    </Fragment>
  </Glance>

  <CaseSection
    num="01"
    title="Session Replay was our #1 request — so I began by defining what great looks like."
  >
    <Prose>
      <h3>What it is</h3>
      <p>
        Session Replay is our number one requested feature from current and
        prospective customers. It provides visual context of what the end user
        was doing alongside the telemetry data of how the application was
        behaving.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>
          It also expands New Relic's capability from traditional app monitoring
          into Digital Experience Monitoring (DEM).
        </strong>
      </p>
      <h3>Understanding the ask</h3>
      <p>
        Before starting, I needed to understand what customers expect from a
        session replay tool — so I ran a competitive analysis, mapping the
        functional landscape and identifying opportunities for differentiation.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <div class="my-8 grid grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      <figure>
        <div
          class="aspect-video overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        >
          <img
            src="/images/session-replay/competitive-1.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            class="h-full w-full object-cover"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Competitive analysis — functional landscape
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <div
          class="aspect-video overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        >
          <img
            src="/images/session-replay/competitive-2.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            class="h-full w-full object-cover object-[0%_90%]"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Competitive analysis — opportunities for differentiation
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <Prose>
      <h3>Jobs-to-be-Done</h3>
      <p>
        I also conducted internal qualitative research with our engineers who
        use Fullstory session replay on a daily basis. The interviews revealed
        they prioritise time-to-insight — specifically the immediate visibility
        of key events on the replay timeline — and they appreciate the ability
        to quickly link errors, the user action, and data together for incident
        triaging. I synthesised these findings into Jobs-to-be-Done statements.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The reproduction:</strong> As a front-end engineer, when a
          JavaScript error occurs, I need to see exactly what the user was doing
          leading up to it, so I can recreate the issue.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The impact:</strong> As a front-end engineer, I need to see
          how the application behaved during the error to determine if this was
          an isolated incident or a critical failure affecting the entire user
          journey.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The root cause:</strong> As a full-stack engineer, I need to
          correlate user actions with back-end technical events on a single
          timeline, so I can pinpoint the source of a bug without switching
          contexts.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="02"
    title="Developers were losing time to context-switching — the fix was one synchronized view."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Troubleshooting means hunting through a sea of fragmented data to
        reconstruct what the user actually did before an error — which creates
        cognitive load and delays time-to-insight. That data lives in three
        disconnected places:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Logs</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Traces</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Timeline</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>

    <Prose class="mt-10">
      <h3>The approach: one synchronized view</h3>
      <p>
        The biggest productivity killer in a debugging flow is context-switching
        — matching a timestamp in a video to a line in a log file. So I unified
        three layers that move together in real time:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Replay player</strong> — the visual experience the user saw</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Event trail</strong> — route changes, JavaScript errors &
          custom events
        </li>
        <li><strong>Session traces</strong> — the underlying logs & network calls</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[1fr_1.3fr] md:items-start">
      <Prose>
        <p>This mirrors how developers actually troubleshoot:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>See the problem in the UI</li>
          <li>Identify the high-level event</li>
          <li>Reach for the granular logs to find the root cause</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Keeping the three layers side-by-side and in sync removes the friction
          of context-switching — letting developers move from{" "}
          <em>what happened</em> to <em>why it happened</em> in one fluid motion.
        </p>
      </Prose>

      <figure class="mx-auto w-4/5">
        <div
          class="overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        >
          <img
            src="/images/session-replay/wireframe.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full scale-110"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Wireframe — unifying replay, event trail & session traces
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="03"
    title="Long sessions are noisy — so the timeline surfaces only the signals that matter."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[1.3fr_1fr] md:items-start">
      <div>
        <Prose>
          <h3>Navigating the noise</h3>
          <p>
            Reviewing session replays is traditionally labour-intensive. By
            overlaying event markers like JavaScript errors and route changes
            onto the timeline, we provide engineers with clear visual cues. Here
            are some explorations around designing a scrubber that doesn't just
            play back a video, but actively surfaces the critical signals,
            allowing engineers to jump straight to the root cause.
          </p>
        </Prose>
        <Callout>
          Surfacing key events on the timeline lets engineers jump straight to
          the root cause — no scrubbing required.
        </Callout>
      </div>
      <figure class="mx-auto w-[85%]">
        <img
          src="/images/session-replay/scrubber.png"
          alt=""
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Scrubber explorations — surfacing critical signals
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-28 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[1.3fr_1fr] md:items-start">
      <div>
        <Prose>
          <h3>Designing for high-density data</h3>
          <p>
            To help users parse long sessions without cognitive fatigue, I
            developed a chapter system that organises dense route changes into a
            scannable timeline. By clustering rapid-fire interactions, we reduce
            visual noise while maintaining a 10px minimum hit-area for
            accessibility. This diagram illustrates three logic models for
            grouping sub-second events — balancing a high-level overview with
            granular accuracy.
          </p>
        </Prose>
        <Callout>
          Grouping dense, rapid-fire events keeps long sessions scannable instead
          of overwhelming.
        </Callout>
      </div>
      <figure class="mx-auto w-[85%]">
        <div
          class="rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        >
          <img
            src="/images/session-replay/route-grouping.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full scale-90"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Three logic models for grouping sub-second route changes
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <Prose class="mt-28">
      <h3>Responsive markers for better legibility</h3>
      <p>
        To ensure the timeline and error markers remain useful on any screen
        size, I designed a merging mechanism for error markers — much like how a
        map groups landmarks as you zoom out. This eagle-eye view automatically
        clusters overlapping errors into a single interactive marker, maintaining
        a clean workspace while preserving the detail within a tooltip.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <div class="my-8 grid grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      <figure>
        <div
          class="aspect-[1676/1098] overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        >
          <img
            src="/images/session-replay/marker-merging.gif"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            class="h-full w-full object-cover"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Error markers merge as space tightens, like landmarks on a map
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/session-replay/chapter-list.png"
          alt=""
          loading="lazy"
          class="aspect-[1676/1098] w-full rounded-2xl object-cover shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Chapter hover list across multiple URLs
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="04"
    title="When testing grouped vs. overlay markers, grouping won on every dimension."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Partnering with the product manager, I ran an internal user test to
        validate this grouped approach against the overlay (no-grouping) approach
        on the timeline. Our goal was to determine which pattern best helps users
        maintain an eagle-eye view of a session in a small space. The findings
        were definitive for the grouped approach:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cognitive ease:</strong> the grouped view was "easier for the
          brain to digest" and "keeps things less cluttered" than the overlay
          approach.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Precision:</strong> participants liked that they could click a
          single square to see exactly what happened in that timeframe without
          overlapping with other error sets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Efficiency in investigation:</strong> the red deepens as more
          errors cluster together, creating a natural heatmap that shows users
          where the most issues occurred and where to start.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
    <figure class="mx-auto mt-12 w-[70%]">
      <div
        class="rounded-2xl bg-card p-3 shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/session-replay/marker-test.png"
          alt=""
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full rounded-lg"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption
        class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
      >
        Marker user test — grouped vs. overlay
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <Callout>
      Grouped markers create a natural heatmap — engineers can see{" "}
      <em>where</em> to start investigating, not just <em>that</em> errors
      occurred.
    </Callout>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="05"
    title="The result isn't a video player — it's a synchronized diagnostic tool."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The final design of New Relic Session Replay is not just a video player
        but a synchronised diagnostic tool. By unifying the video player (with
        timeline scrubber) and the Event Trail and Session Trace components, I
        ensured that every technical signal is perfectly in sync with the user's
        visual journey. It lets developers see an error and the user's behaviour
        side-by-side, at the exact moment it happened. This balance of granular
        detail and intuitive context is what ultimately{" "}
        <strong>cuts time-to-insight from minutes to seconds.</strong>
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <figure class="mx-auto mt-16 w-[70%]">
      <img
        src="/images/session-replay/final.gif"
        alt=""
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
      />
      <figcaption
        class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
      >
        The final synchronised diagnostic experience
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    num="06"
    title="The impact: a new revenue pillar, and troubleshooting in seconds."
  >
    <Prose>
      <p>
        Session Replay scaled to <strong>1,000+ active accounts</strong> and{" "}
        <strong>7M weekly sessions</strong> within six months of launch,
        generating <strong>$3.52M in monthly recurring revenue</strong> and
        establishing it as a core pillar of New Relic's Browser capability.
      </p>
      <p>
        By giving developers a single source of truth, it eliminated
        context-switching — turning a symptom into a diagnosed root cause in one
        fluid motion.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <p class="mt-6 font-medium text-[#0041d9]">
      → Time-to-insight dropped from minutes to seconds.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
